// Requirements from every file of one batch, into a fresh RG project: one CC call per file, in parallel, and
// ONE chain of snapshots. The batch record is deleted from the meta table once every file's requirements are in.
//
// A temporary RG project is created - for real, in development runs too - with the given description and an RG
// genesis pipeline. One record of a meta table (the output of a dir-batcher run) lists the files, one absolute
// path per line. mh.batch-line-splitter gives every file a branch of its own: read the file, ask CC for its
// requirements, check CC's answer. The branches converge, their answers are collected, one Task stores every
// requirement in the project, and only then is the batch record deleted.
//
// Reusable by construction - nothing about a particular run appears below:
//   rgBaseUrl           comes IN, the RG to work in, e.g. http://localhost:64967
//   locale              comes IN, the project's language
//   projectDescription  comes IN, what the requirements are for; the project's description and part of every
//                       prompt
//   rgPipelineUid       comes IN, the RG genesis pipeline the project runs, e.g. mhdg-rg-cc-1.0.76
//   metaTable           comes IN, the meta-storage type holding the batch records
//   batchKey            comes IN, the record to process and then delete, e.g. batch-0004
//   synthetic           comes IN, 'true' reads and deletes in MH_META_STORAGE_SYNTHETIC, 'false' in MH_META_STORAGE.
//                       The delete refuses anything but exactly true or false
//   production          comes IN, REQUIRED by convention. It gates nothing here: the project is created and filled
//                       in every run, and which table is read is 'synthetic'
//   projectCode, reqIds, reqSources go OUT at ExecContext level
//
// WHY THE STORE IS NOT IN THE BRANCHES. Every requirement goes into one project, and RG's requirements/manual
// forks a new STAGE from whichever COMMITTED snapshot it is given - it does not refuse a parent that already has
// children. Branches storing in parallel would fork the project into one leaf per file, and only one of them
// could have run the one-shot requirements/manual/first at all. So a branch only produces its checked answer,
// mh.aggregate collects the answers by name across the ExecContext, and one Task stores them as a single chain.
//
// EVERY FILE OR NOTHING. A branch that fails holds its file back: the answers no longer cover the batch, the
// store refuses before RG is touched, and the batch record stays in the queue. Resetting the failed Task re-runs
// that file alone; the other files' answers are kept.
//
// Launch with mh_create_exec_context_with_variables; mh_create_exec_context refuses a SourceCode that declares
// source-level inputs (562.120).
//
// ORDER is declaration order: each process is chained to the one declared before it, and everything after the
// splitter waits for all of its branches.
//
// 1.1: cc is cached - cache on, cacheMeta. The key is the Function code, the SHA-256 and length of the prompt, and
// every meta of the process (timeout-sec, and model when one is set); the Function's git revision is not part of
// it. A re-run of a batch asks CC only about files whose prompt changed or whose answer was never stored. Only a
// run of a version that declares the cache writes to it, so nothing a 1.0 run answered is in it.
// A reset of a cached Task answers from the cache (CHECK_CACHE): an answer the check step refuses comes back on a
// plain reset of cc. Drop the entry first with the Task's reset-cache (POST /exec-context/task-reset-cache).
// 1.1: cc runs mh.asset.call-cc_1.1 - the same payload, plus the analyzer that lets the execution gate identify a CC
// session limit: call-cc is withheld for a while and the Task retries for free, instead of spending its tries.
// Import the call-cc bundle before this one, so the code is registered when this graph is validated.
// 1.2: dropRecord is COMMENTED OUT - for now the batch record stays in the meta table after the run, so the same
// batch can be run again. The graph ends at store. Restore the process (and bump the uid) to consume the queue again.

// 1.3: paths is cached (cache on) - the same batch record answers the same list of paths from the cache.

// 1.4: select is cached (cache on) - same batchKey + metaTable + synthetic always returns the same record body.

// 1.5: mkproject is handed the run's model and effort too, not only cc. Without them the project was created with no
// pair, and RG recorded RgLlmModel.DEFAULT / RgLlmEffort.DEFAULT - Opus 4.8 / High - on the project and on every
// snapshot resolved from it, whichever model the run pinned for cc. mh.asset.rg-temp-project_1.1 sends both to
// RgProjectController.createProject when they are bound; unseeded or nullified, RG's default still applies.
// store is handed them too: the requirements it writes were produced by cc with that pair, so it sends the pair to
// requirements/manual/first (the genesis) and to mhdg_rg_open_stage (the STAGE the rest go into) - every snapshot the
// run seals records the pair that wrote its content. Needs the RG build whose mhdg_rg_open_stage accepts model/effort.
source "mh-rg-requirements-from-batch-1.5" {

    variables {
        <- rgBaseUrl
        <- locale
        <- projectDescription
        <- rgPipelineUid
        <- metaTable
        <- batchKey
        <- synthetic
        <- production
        // model and effort are OPTIONAL ExecContext-level inputs, like synthetic - a property of the RUN, not of
        // this SourceCode. Seed them to pin what CC uses (DAHF 0.5: opus 4.8 / medium for development); leave
        // either unseeded and it reaches cc as mh.null-value, which mh.asset.call-cc reads as "omit the flag",
        // so CC falls back to its own default. Nullable init: mh_create_exec_context_with_variables may pass
        // "mh.null-value" for either, or omit it entirely.
        <- model?
        <- effort?
        -> projectCode
        -> reqIds
        -> reqSources
    }

    mkproject := mh.asset.rg-temp-project_1.1 {
        name "Create the temporary RG project that will hold the requirements"
        meta variable-for-rg-base-url = "rgBaseUrl",
             variable-for-locale = "locale",
             variable-for-production = "production",
             variable-for-project-code = "projectCode",
             variable-for-description = "projectDescription",
             variable-for-rg-pipeline-uid = "rgPipelineUid",
             variable-for-model = "model",
             variable-for-effort = "effort",
             create-in-development = "true"
        <- rgBaseUrl, locale, production, projectDescription, rgPipelineUid, model?, effort?
        -> projectCode: ext=".txt"
        timeout 60
    }

    select := internal mh.meta-storage {
        name "Read the batch record"
        meta action = "select",
             type = "metaTable",
             keys = "batchKey",
             synthetic = "synthetic",
             output = "batchRecords"
        <- metaTable, batchKey, synthetic
        -> batchRecords: ext=".json"
        timeout 120
        cache on
    }

    paths := mh.asset.rg-batch-paths_1.0 {
        name "Turn the batch record into its list of paths"
        meta variable-for-records = "batchRecords",
             variable-for-paths = "batchPaths"
        <- batchRecords
        -> batchPaths: ext=".txt"
        timeout 60
        cache on
    }

    split := internal mh.batch-line-splitter {
        name "One branch per file of the batch"
        meta number-of-lines-per-task = "1",
             variable-for-splitting = "batchPaths",
             output-is-dynamic = "true",
             output-variable = "sourcePath",
             is-array = "false"
        sequential {
            prompt := mh.asset.rg-req-path-prompt_1.0 {
                name "Read the file and build the CC prompt"
                meta variable-for-source-path = "sourcePath",
                     variable-for-description = "projectDescription",
                     variable-for-prompt = "ccPrompt"
                <- sourcePath, projectDescription
                -> ccPrompt: ext=".txt"
                timeout 60
            }

            cc := mh.asset.call-cc_1.1 {
                name "Ask CC for the file's requirements"
                // the process timeout must be larger than timeout-sec, or MH kills the Task before it can say why
                meta variable-for-prompt = "ccPrompt",
                     variable-for-output = "ccResult",
                     variable-for-model = "model",
                     variable-for-effort = "effort",
                     timeout-sec = "900"
                <- ccPrompt, model?, effort?
                -> ccResult: ext=".txt"
                timeout 1200
                tries 2
                // 1.1: model and effort are optional inputs (see the variables block), not literals - a run pins
                // the DAHF 0.5 development pairing (opus 4.8 / medium) by seeding them, or omits either for CC's
                // default. As inputs their content is hashed into the cache key, so changing the model or effort a
                // run uses correctly misses the old entry; cacheMeta additionally keeps the prompt and timeout apart.
                cache on, cacheMeta
            }

            check := mh.asset.rg-req-check_1.0 {
                name "Check CC's answer and hand it on as one line"
                meta variable-for-source-path = "sourcePath",
                     variable-for-cc-result = "ccResult",
                     variable-for-answer = "reqAnswer"
                <- sourcePath, ccResult
                -> reqAnswer: ext=".txt"
                timeout 60
            }
        }
    }

    gather := internal mh.aggregate {
        name "Collect every file's checked answer"
        // collects by NAME across the whole ExecContext - a branch's variable is invisible at the top level
        meta variables = "reqAnswer",
             type = "text",
             produce-metadata = "false",
             error-control-policy = "fail"
        -> reqAnswers: ext=".txt"
        timeout 300
    }

    store := mh.asset.rg-req-store-batch_1.0 {
        name "Store every file's requirements in the project, as one chain"
        // requirement #1 waits for the project's genesis run (up to 900s), each next one is one transaction
        meta variable-for-rg-base-url = "rgBaseUrl",
             variable-for-project-code = "projectCode",
             variable-for-paths = "batchPaths",
             variable-for-answers = "reqAnswers",
             variable-for-req-ids = "reqIds",
             variable-for-req-sources = "reqSources",
             variable-for-model = "model",
             variable-for-effort = "effort"
        <- rgBaseUrl, projectCode, batchPaths, reqAnswers, model?, effort?
        -> reqIds: ext=".txt",
           reqSources: ext=".txt"
        timeout 14400
    }

    // 1.2: disabled - the batch record is left in the table for now (see the header)
    // dropRecord := internal mh.meta-storage {
    //     name "Take the processed batch record off the queue"
    //     meta action = "delete",
    //          type = "metaTable",
    //          keys = "batchKey",
    //          synthetic = "synthetic"
    //     <- metaTable, batchKey, synthetic
    //     timeout 60
    // }
}
